WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.460 --> 00:07.540
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

00:09.290 --> 00:13.590
Good afternoon.

00:13.590 --> 00:21.860
The Federal Open Market Committee concluded a two-day meeting earlier today.

00:21.860 --> 00:25.260
Based on its review of recent economic and financial developments,

00:25.260 --> 00:29.200
the Committee sees the economy continuing to grow at a moderate pace,

00:29.200 --> 00:34.440
notwithstanding the strong headwinds created by current federal fiscal policies.

00:34.440 --> 00:36.230
The labor market has continued to improve,

00:36.230 --> 00:38.480
with gains in private payroll employment averaging

00:38.480 --> 00:43.060
about 200,000 jobs per month over the past six months.

00:43.060 --> 00:45.580
Job gains, along with the strengthening housing market,

00:45.580 --> 00:48.910
have in turn contributed to increases in consumer confidence

00:48.910 --> 00:51.780
and supported household spending.

00:51.780 --> 00:55.470
However, at 7.6 percent, the unemployment rate remains elevated,

00:55.470 --> 01:00.430
as do rates of underemployment and long-term unemployment.

01:00.430 --> 01:03.120
Overall, the Committee believes the downside risks to the outlook

01:03.120 --> 01:06.280
for the economy and the labor market have diminished since the fall,

01:06.280 --> 01:11.320
but we will continue to evaluate economic conditions and risks as they evolve.

01:12.720 --> 01:15.440
Inflation has been running below the Committee's longer-run objective

01:15.440 --> 01:19.850
of 2 percent for some time and has been a bit softer recently.

01:19.850 --> 01:22.770
The Committee believes that the recent softness partly reflects transitory

01:22.770 --> 01:26.870
factors, and with longer-term inflation expectations remaining stable,

01:26.870 --> 01:28.130
the Committee expects inflation

01:28.130 --> 01:32.130
to move back towards this 2 percent longer-term objective over time.

01:32.130 --> 01:36.220
We will, however, be closely monitoring these developments as well.

01:37.300 --> 01:38.540
In conjunction with this meeting,

01:38.540 --> 01:42.480
the 19 participants in our policy discussion-the 7 Board members

01:42.480 --> 01:47.740
and the 12 Reserve Bank presidents-submitted individual economic projections.

01:47.740 --> 01:52.040
As always, each participant's projections are conditioned on his or her own view

01:52.040 --> 01:55.120
of appropriate monetary policy.

01:55.120 --> 01:58.570
Generally, the projections of individual participants show they expect moderate

01:58.570 --> 02:02.570
growth, picking up over time, and gradual progress towards levels

02:02.570 --> 02:04.560
of unemployment and inflation consistent

02:04.560 --> 02:07.000
with the Federal Reserve's statutory mandate

02:07.000 --> 02:11.320
to foster maximum employment and price stability.

02:11.320 --> 02:15.460
In brief, participants' projections for economic growth have a central tendency

02:15.460 --> 02:23.620
of 2.3 to 2.6 percent for 2013, rising to 2.9 to 3.6 percent in 2015.

02:23.620 --> 02:26.120
The central tendency of their projections of the unemployment rate

02:26.120 --> 02:31.030
for the fourth quarter of this year is 7.2 to 7.3 percent,

02:31.030 --> 02:36.750
declining to 5.8 to 6.2 percent in the final quarter of 2015.

02:36.750 --> 02:39.130
Most participants see inflation gradually increasing

02:39.130 --> 02:42.960
from its current low level toward the Committee's longer-run objective;

02:42.960 --> 02:47.660
the central tendency of their projections for inflation is 0.8 to 1.2 percent

02:47.660 --> 02:54.640
for this year and 1.6 to 2.0 percent for 2015.

02:54.640 --> 02:56.630
Before turning to today's policy decision,

02:56.630 --> 02:59.110
let me say a few words about the Federal Reserve's strategy

02:59.110 --> 03:02.460
for normalizing policy in the long run.

03:02.460 --> 03:06.400
In the minutes of its June 2011 meeting, the Committee set forth principles

03:06.400 --> 03:11.110
that it intended to follow when the time came to normalize policy and the size

03:11.110 --> 03:13.630
and the structure of the Federal Reserve's balance sheet.

03:13.630 --> 03:15.840
As part of prudent planning,

03:15.840 --> 03:19.250
we have been reviewing these principles in recent meetings.

03:19.250 --> 03:21.130
We expect those discussions to continue

03:21.130 --> 03:25.200
and intend to provide further information at an appropriate time.

03:25.200 --> 03:28.200
For today, I will note that, in the view of most participants,

03:28.200 --> 03:32.330
the broad principles set out in June 2011 remain applicable.

03:32.330 --> 03:34.860
One difference is worth mentioning.

03:34.860 --> 03:37.710
While participants continue to think that, in the long run,

03:37.710 --> 03:40.160
the Federal Reserve's portfolio should consist predominantly

03:40.160 --> 03:43.710
of Treasury securities, a strong majority now expects

03:43.710 --> 03:47.330
that the Committee will not sell agency mortgage-backed securities during the

03:47.330 --> 03:51.170
process of normalizing monetary policy, although in the longer run,

03:51.170 --> 03:56.060
limited sales could be used to reduce or eliminate residual MBS holdings.

03:56.060 --> 03:59.150
I emphasize that, given the outlook and the Committee's policy guidance,

03:59.150 --> 04:03.970
these matters are unlikely to be relevant to actual policy for quite a while.

04:03.970 --> 04:06.720
Let me turn now to current policy issues.

04:06.720 --> 04:08.640
With unemployment still elevated

04:08.640 --> 04:11.720
and inflation below the Committee's longer-run objective,

04:11.720 --> 04:15.580
the Committee is continuing its highly accommodative policies.

04:15.580 --> 04:19.550
As you know, in normal times, the Committee eases monetary policy

04:19.550 --> 04:22.090
by lowering the target for the short-term policy interest rate,

04:22.090 --> 04:24.130
the federal funds rate.

04:24.130 --> 04:26.210
However, the target range for the federal funds rate,

04:26.210 --> 04:31.480
currently at 0 to ¼ percent, cannot meaningfully be reduced further.

04:31.480 --> 04:35.390
Thus, we are providing policy accommodation through two alternative methods:

04:35.390 --> 04:38.090
first, by communicating to the public the Committee's plans

04:38.090 --> 04:42.140
for setting the federal funds rate target over the medium term, and, second,

04:42.140 --> 04:44.600
by purchasing and holding Treasury securities

04:44.600 --> 04:47.210
and agency mortgage-backed securities.

04:47.210 --> 04:52.400
Let me discuss a few key points regarding each of these two policy tools.

04:52.400 --> 04:55.900
First, today the Committee reaffirmed its expectation

04:55.900 --> 04:59.720
that the current exceptionally low range for the funds rate will be appropriate

04:59.720 --> 05:03.940
at least as long as the unemployment rate remains above 6½ percent so long

05:03.940 --> 05:07.190
as inflation and inflation expectations remain well behaved

05:07.190 --> 05:09.500
in the senses described in the FOMC statement.

05:09.500 --> 05:13.390
As I have noted frequently, the phrase "at least as long"

05:13.390 --> 05:16.400
in the Committee's interest rate guidance is important.

05:16.400 --> 05:17.730
The economic conditions we have set

05:17.730 --> 05:22.970
out as preceding any future rate increase are thresholds, not triggers.

05:22.970 --> 05:26.540
For example, assuming that inflation is near our objective at that time,

05:26.540 --> 05:29.140
as expected, a decline in the unemployment rate

05:29.140 --> 05:32.750
to 6½ percent would not lead automatically to an increase

05:32.750 --> 05:34.650
in the federal funds rate target,

05:34.650 --> 05:37.280
but rather would indicate only that it was appropriate for the Committee

05:37.280 --> 05:43.230
to consider whether the broader economic outlook justified such an increase.

05:43.230 --> 05:46.550
All else equal, the more subdued the outlook for inflation at that time,

05:46.550 --> 05:50.410
the more patient the Committee would likely be in making that assessment.

05:50.410 --> 05:53.390
In the projections submitted for this meeting,

05:53.390 --> 05:58.380
14 of 19 FOMC participants indicated that they expect the first increase

05:58.380 --> 06:02.370
in the target for the federal funds rate to occur in 2015,

06:02.370 --> 06:06.830
and one expected the first increase to incur in 2016.

06:06.830 --> 06:09.840
Moreover, so long as the economy remains short of maximum employment,

06:09.840 --> 06:12.470
inflation remains near our longer-run objective,

06:12.470 --> 06:15.430
and inflation expectations remain well anchored,

06:15.430 --> 06:18.890
increases in the target for the federal funds rate, once they begin,

06:18.890 --> 06:22.620
are likely to be gradual, consistent with the Committee's balanced approach

06:22.620 --> 06:25.530
to meeting its employment and price stability objectives.

06:25.530 --> 06:30.080
The purpose of this forward guidance about policy is to assure households

06:30.080 --> 06:34.260
and businesses that monetary policy will continue to support the recovery even

06:34.260 --> 06:38.760
as the pace of economic growth and job creation picks up.

06:38.760 --> 06:40.330
Importantly, as our statement notes,

06:40.330 --> 06:43.370
the Committee expects a considerable interval of time to pass

06:43.370 --> 06:45.780
between when the Committee will cease adding accommodation

06:45.780 --> 06:48.710
through asset purchases and the time when the Committee will begin

06:48.710 --> 06:52.310
to reduce accommodation by moving the federal funds rate target toward more

06:52.310 --> 06:53.610
normal levels.

06:53.610 --> 06:58.930
The second policy tool being employed by the Committee is asset purchases.

06:58.930 --> 07:02.520
Specifically, the Committee has been purchasing $40 billion per month

07:02.520 --> 07:04.710
in agency mortgage-backed securities

07:04.710 --> 07:08.920
and $45 billion per month in Treasury securities.

07:08.920 --> 07:11.730
When our program of asset purchases was initiated last September,

07:11.730 --> 07:15.160
the Committee stated the goal of promoting a substantial improvement

07:15.160 --> 07:19.220
in the outlook for the labor market in the context of price stability

07:19.220 --> 07:21.440
and noted it would also be taking appropriate account

07:21.440 --> 07:24.600
of the efficacy and costs of the program.

07:24.600 --> 07:27.670
Today the Committee made no changes to the purchase program.

07:27.670 --> 07:32.890
Although the Committee left the pace of purchases unchanged at today's meeting,

07:32.890 --> 07:35.720
it has stated that it may vary the pace of purchases

07:35.720 --> 07:38.420
as economic conditions evolve.

07:38.420 --> 07:41.650
Any such change would reflect the incoming data and their implications

07:41.650 --> 07:45.290
for the outlook, as well as the cumulative progress made toward the Committee's

07:45.290 --> 07:49.410
objectives since the program began in September.

07:49.410 --> 07:51.980
Going forward, the economic outcomes that the Committee sees

07:51.980 --> 07:55.990
as most likely involve continuing gains in labor markets,

07:55.990 --> 07:59.600
supported by moderate growth that picks up over the next several quarters

07:59.600 --> 08:05.320
as the near-term restraint from fiscal policy and other headwinds diminishes.

08:05.320 --> 08:09.800
We also see inflation moving back toward our 2 percent objective over time.

08:09.800 --> 08:13.470
If the incoming data are broadly consistent with this forecast,

08:13.470 --> 08:16.340
the Committee currently anticipates that it would be appropriate

08:16.340 --> 08:19.710
to moderate the monthly pace of purchases later this year.

08:19.710 --> 08:23.040
And if the subsequent data remain broadly aligned with our current expectations

08:23.040 --> 08:26.330
for the economy, we would continue to reduce the pace of purchases

08:26.330 --> 08:29.800
in measured steps through the first half of next year,

08:29.800 --> 08:32.750
ending purchases around midyear.

08:32.750 --> 08:36.210
In this scenario, when asset purchases ultimately come to an end,

08:36.210 --> 08:39.900
the unemployment rate would likely be in the vicinity of 7 percent,

08:39.900 --> 08:43.270
with solid economic growth supporting further job gains,

08:43.270 --> 08:47.260
a substantial improvement from the 8.1 percent unemployment rate that prevailed

08:47.260 --> 08:48.880
when the Committee announced this program.

08:48.880 --> 08:53.730
I would like to emphasize once more the point that our policy is

08:53.730 --> 08:57.990
in no way predetermined and will depend on the incoming data and the evolution

08:57.990 --> 09:02.600
of the outlook as well as on the cumulative progress toward our objectives.

09:02.600 --> 09:04.580
If conditions improve faster than expected,

09:04.580 --> 09:08.590
the pace of asset purchases could be reduced somewhat more quickly.

09:08.590 --> 09:11.800
If the outlook becomes less favorable, on the other hand,

09:11.800 --> 09:15.440
or if financial conditions are judged to be inconsistent with further progress

09:15.440 --> 09:19.950
in the labor markets, reductions in the pace of purchases could be delayed.

09:19.950 --> 09:23.580
Indeed, should it be needed, the Committee would be prepared to employ all

09:23.580 --> 09:27.830
of its tools, including an increase in the pace of purchases for a time,

09:27.830 --> 09:32.210
to promote a return to maximum employment in a context of price stability.

09:32.210 --> 09:36.670
It's also worth noting here that, even if a modest reduction in the pace

09:36.670 --> 09:40.160
of asset purchases occurs, we would not be shrinking the Federal Reserve's

09:40.160 --> 09:43.660
portfolio of securities, but only slowing the pace at which we are adding

09:43.660 --> 09:47.170
to the portfolio while continuing to reinvest principal payments

09:47.170 --> 09:50.860
and proceeds from maturing holdings as well.

09:50.860 --> 09:53.800
These large and growing holdings will continue to put downward pressure

09:53.800 --> 09:56.030
on longer-term interest rates.

09:56.030 --> 09:58.700
To use the analogy of driving an automobile,

09:58.700 --> 10:01.470
any slowing in the pace of purchases will be akin to letting up a bit

10:01.470 --> 10:04.360
on the gas pedal as the car picks up speed,

10:04.360 --> 10:06.580
not to beginning to apply the brakes.

10:06.580 --> 10:10.790
I will close by drawing again the important distinction

10:10.790 --> 10:14.370
between the Committee's decisions about adjusting the pace of asset purchases

10:14.370 --> 10:18.620
and its forward guidance regarding the target for the federal funds rate.

10:18.620 --> 10:21.340
As I mentioned, the current level of the federal funds rate target is likely

10:21.340 --> 10:23.510
to remain appropriate for a considerable period

10:23.510 --> 10:27.350
after asset purchases are concluded.

10:27.350 --> 10:30.980
To return to the driving analogy, if the incoming data support the view

10:30.980 --> 10:34.070
that the economy is able to sustain a reasonable cruising speed,

10:34.070 --> 10:36.150
we will ease the pressure on the accelerator

10:36.150 --> 10:39.470
by gradually reducing the pace of purchases.

10:39.470 --> 10:42.630
However, any need to consider applying the brakes

10:42.630 --> 10:46.860
by raising short-term rates is still far in the future.

10:46.860 --> 10:49.670
In any case, no matter how conditions may evolve,

10:49.670 --> 10:52.980
the Federal Reserve remains committed to fostering substantial improvement

10:52.980 --> 10:57.270
in the outlook for the labor market in a context of price stability.

10:57.270 --> 10:57.640
Thank you.

10:57.640 --> 10:59.220
I'd be glad to take your questions.

10:59.220 --> 11:00.580
STEVE LIESMAN.

11:00.580 --> 11:03.070
Steve Liesman with CNBC.

11:03.070 --> 11:07.570
I hate to use my question to ask you to clarify something,

11:07.570 --> 11:14.630
but when you said gradually reduce purchases, beginning later this year

11:14.630 --> 11:19.920
and ending it next year when the unemployment rate hits 7 percent-what is that?

11:19.920 --> 11:22.380
Is that a decision by the FOMC?

11:22.380 --> 11:26.220
Is that an intention that- CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

11:26.220 --> 11:31.240
That is-we had a good discussion of that issue today,

11:31.240 --> 11:35.370
and we haven't had-obviously, there's no change,

11:35.370 --> 11:38.960
there's no change in policy involved here, there's simply a clarification,

11:38.960 --> 11:43.300
helping people to think about where policy will evolve.

11:43.300 --> 11:45.440
So, it was thought that it might be best for me to explain

11:45.440 --> 11:48.420
that to this group and answer questions.

11:48.420 --> 11:50.780
Future policy statements may include elements of this,

11:50.780 --> 11:52.430
but it's not a policy change.

11:52.430 --> 11:57.040
I'm just trying to explain how are-how we're making a substantial improvement

11:57.040 --> 11:59.290
in the outlook for the labor market, how we're making that concrete,

11:59.290 --> 12:03.390
and how we're thinking about the potential future

12:03.390 --> 12:08.000
of the program given alternative policy and economic developments.

12:08.000 --> 12:08.420
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:08.420 --> 12:09.860
So there is no consensus on that?

12:09.860 --> 12:11.420
That's not a vote of the FOMC,

12:11.420 --> 12:14.580
that's not a plan that's written down some place or-?

12:14.580 --> 12:14.990
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

12:14.990 --> 12:18.650
It represents the consensus of the FOMC, yes.

12:18.650 --> 12:19.180
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:19.180 --> 12:21.690
If you could just-the question I was going to ask was,

12:21.690 --> 12:25.320
could you give us some information on "substantial improvement"?

12:25.320 --> 12:29.540
Is that the unemployment rate coming down by itself to 7 percent,

12:29.540 --> 12:31.270
or are there other factors involved,

12:31.270 --> 12:33.590
and is it "substantial" compared to the fall?

12:33.590 --> 12:35.060
Is that when- CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

12:35.060 --> 12:39.000
Well, there are many factors that we look at when trying

12:39.000 --> 12:40.300
to judge the state of the labor market.

12:40.300 --> 12:45.270
As you know, we look at participation, payrolls, a variety of other data,

12:45.270 --> 12:50.310
but the 7 percent unemployment rate is indicative of the kind of progress we'd

12:50.310 --> 12:55.120
like to make in order to be able to say that we've reached substantial progress.

12:55.120 --> 12:57.880
JON HILSENRATH.

12:59.490 --> 13:02.590
Jon Hilsenrath from the Wall Street Journal.

13:02.590 --> 13:06.810
Mr. Chairman, there's an undercurrent of optimism in your forecast

13:06.810 --> 13:09.370
and your statement-in the policy statement today.

13:09.370 --> 13:11.360
For instance, the unemployment rate forecast comes

13:11.360 --> 13:16.360
down to 6.5 to 6.8 percent next year.

13:16.360 --> 13:20.980
It's the case that the Fed has overestimated the economy's growth rate very

13:20.980 --> 13:27.850
often in the past during this recovery, so-and we've gone through a period

13:27.850 --> 13:30.140
in the first half of the year with pretty subdued growth.

13:30.140 --> 13:36.310
So I would like to hear you explain where this optimism comes from

13:36.310 --> 13:40.880
and how confident you are that these expectations are going to be met.

13:40.880 --> 13:42.310
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

13:42.310 --> 13:45.470
Well, the fundamentals look a little better to us.

13:45.470 --> 13:49.850
In particular, the housing sector, which has been a drag on growth

13:49.850 --> 13:54.680
since the crisis, is now, obviously, a support to growth.

13:54.680 --> 13:58.780
It's not only creating construction jobs, but as house prices rise,

13:58.780 --> 14:02.630
increased household wealth supports consumption spending, consumer sentiment.

14:02.630 --> 14:06.240
State and local governments, who have been a major drag,

14:06.240 --> 14:09.070
are now coming to a position where they no longer have

14:09.070 --> 14:11.470
to lay off large numbers of workers.

14:11.470 --> 14:15.050
Generally speaking, financial conditions are improving.

14:15.050 --> 14:20.400
The main drag, or the main headwind, to growth this year is, as you know,

14:20.400 --> 14:25.340
is the federal fiscal policy, which the CBO estimates is something on the order

14:25.340 --> 14:27.530
of 1½ percentage points of growth.

14:27.530 --> 14:31.080
And our judgment is that, you know, given that very heavy headwind,

14:31.080 --> 14:35.320
the fact that the economy is still moving ahead at at least a moderate pace,

14:35.320 --> 14:40.780
is indicative that the underlying factors are improving.

14:40.780 --> 14:42.510
And so we'll see how that evolves.

14:42.510 --> 14:47.130
Obviously, we haven't seen the full effect yet of the fiscal policy changes;

14:47.130 --> 14:52.360
we'll want to see how that evolves as we get through that fiscal impact.

14:52.360 --> 14:55.850
But we're hopeful, as you can see from the individual projections-and again,

14:55.850 --> 14:57.140
these are individual projections,

14:57.140 --> 15:02.080
not an official forecast of the Committee-we'll be obviously very interested

15:02.080 --> 15:04.410
to see if the economy does pick up a bit

15:04.410 --> 15:09.340
and continue to reduce unemployment, as we anticipate.

15:09.340 --> 15:12.090
I think one thing that's very important for me to say is that,

15:12.090 --> 15:15.200
if you draw the conclusion that I've just said,

15:15.200 --> 15:19.500
that our policies-that our purchases will end in the middle of next year,

15:19.500 --> 15:23.090
you've drawn the wrong conclusion because our purchases are tied

15:23.090 --> 15:24.770
to what happens in the economy.

15:24.770 --> 15:27.330
And if the Federal Reserve makes the same error

15:27.330 --> 15:30.900
and we overestimate what's happening, then our policies will adjust to that.

15:30.900 --> 15:34.650
We are not-we have no deterministic or fixed plan.

15:34.650 --> 15:38.630
Rather, our policies are going to depend on this scenario coming true.

15:38.630 --> 15:45.340
If it doesn't come true, we'll adjust our policies to that.

15:45.340 --> 15:48.230
ALISTER BULL.

15:48.230 --> 15:49.700
Alister Bull from Reuters.

15:49.700 --> 15:51.440
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

15:51.440 --> 15:54.190
Financial conditions have tightened in the last few weeks

15:54.190 --> 15:57.490
and bond yields have gone up, and they've gone up again today.

15:57.490 --> 15:58.880
Why do you think that is?

15:58.880 --> 16:02.660
And could you talk a little bit about whether that rise in bond yields

16:02.660 --> 16:06.600
and interest-longer-term interest rates could affect your economic outlook and,

16:06.600 --> 16:10.060
particularly, given that mortgage rates are now back up above 4 percent,

16:10.060 --> 16:12.960
if that could affect the recovery under way in the housing market?

16:12.960 --> 16:13.600
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

16:13.600 --> 16:14.590
Well, that's a good question.

16:14.590 --> 16:16.790
Yes, rates have come up some.

16:16.790 --> 16:21.310
That's in part due to more optimism, I think, about the economy.

16:21.310 --> 16:25.910
It's in part due to perceptions of the Federal Reserve.

16:25.910 --> 16:32.290
The forecast-the projections that our participants submitted for this meeting,

16:32.290 --> 16:35.850
of course, were done in the last few days, so they were done with full knowledge

16:35.850 --> 16:38.280
of what had happened to financial conditions.

16:38.280 --> 16:41.120
Rates have tightened some, but, you know,

16:41.120 --> 16:45.060
other factors have been more positive-increasing house prices, for example.

16:45.060 --> 16:47.840
I think as far as the housing market is concerned,

16:47.840 --> 16:52.410
we're going to want to watch that, but one important difference now is

16:52.410 --> 16:55.060
that people are more optimistic about housing.

16:55.060 --> 16:58.660
They expect house prices to continue to rise, and we see that, for example,

16:58.660 --> 17:01.880
in a survey question in the Michigan survey.

17:01.880 --> 17:04.390
And that, you know, compensates to some extent

17:04.390 --> 17:06.800
for a slightly higher mortgage rate.

17:06.800 --> 17:10.040
And, in fact, in terms of monthly payments on an average house,

17:10.040 --> 17:13.790
the change in mortgage rates we've seen so far is not all that dramatic.

17:13.790 --> 17:18.950
So, yes, our forecast-our projections do factor that in,

17:18.950 --> 17:24.140
and if interest rates go up for the right reasons-that is,

17:24.140 --> 17:28.930
both optimism about the economy and an accurate assessment

17:28.930 --> 17:33.050
of monetary policy-that's a good thing.

17:33.050 --> 17:40.520
That's not a bad thing.

17:40.520 --> 17:40.960
ROBIN HARDING.

17:40.960 --> 17:43.760
Robin Harding from the Financial Times.

17:43.760 --> 17:47.660
Mr. Chairman, you've always argued that it's the stock of assets

17:47.660 --> 17:51.650
that the Federal Reserve holds which affects long-term interest rates.

17:51.650 --> 17:56.040
How do you reconcile that with a very sharp rise in real interest rates

17:56.040 --> 17:57.930
that we've seen in recent weeks?

17:57.930 --> 18:02.100
And do you think the market is correctly interpreting what you think is most

18:02.100 --> 18:06.540
likely to be the future path of the Federal Reserve's stock of assets?

18:06.540 --> 18:07.260
Thank you.

18:07.260 --> 18:08.960
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

18:08.960 --> 18:12.130
Well, we were a little puzzled by that.

18:12.130 --> 18:17.740
It was bigger than can be explained I think by changes in the ultimate stock

18:17.740 --> 18:21.420
of asset purchases within reasonable ranges.

18:21.420 --> 18:24.640
So I think we have to conclude that there are other factors at work as well,

18:24.640 --> 18:28.080
including, again, some optimism about the economy,

18:28.080 --> 18:31.650
maybe some uncertainty arising.

18:31.650 --> 18:36.440
So, I'm agreeing with you that it seems larger than can be explained

18:36.440 --> 18:40.440
by a changing view of monetary policy.

18:40.440 --> 18:46.310
It's difficult to judge whether the markets are in sync or not.

18:46.310 --> 18:51.980
Generally speaking though, I think that what I've seen from analysts

18:51.980 --> 18:55.800
and market participants is not wildly different from what, you know,

18:55.800 --> 18:59.310
the Committee is thinking and trying to-as I try today to communicate.

18:59.310 --> 19:02.800
I think the most important thing that I just want to convey, again,

19:02.800 --> 19:08.780
is that it's important not to say "this date, that date, this time,"

19:08.780 --> 19:14.540
it's important to understand that our policies are economic dependent and,

19:14.540 --> 19:17.800
in particular, if financial conditions move in a way

19:17.800 --> 19:22.800
that make this economic scenario unlikely, for example,

19:22.800 --> 19:26.140
then that would be a reason for us to adjust our policy.

19:32.410 --> 19:33.640
YLAN MUI. Thanks.

19:33.640 --> 19:35.630
Ylan Mui, Washington Post.

19:35.630 --> 19:39.250
On Monday, President Obama said in an interview that he believed

19:39.250 --> 19:42.850
that you had stayed in your position as chairman for longer than you wanted to

19:42.850 --> 19:44.270
and maybe longer than you were supposed to.

19:44.270 --> 19:47.620
Do you agree with that assessment of your term,

19:47.620 --> 19:50.460
and can you update us on any conversations you've had

19:50.460 --> 19:51.590
with him about your future?

19:51.590 --> 19:52.780
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

19:52.780 --> 19:56.490
Well, Ylan, we just spent two days working on monetary policy issues,

19:56.490 --> 20:00.370
and I would like to keep the debate, discussion, the questions here on policy.

20:00.370 --> 20:03.730
I don't have anything for you on my personal plans.

20:03.730 --> 20:06.810
CRAIG TORRES.

20:06.810 --> 20:10.910
Greetings, Mr. Chairman.

20:10.910 --> 20:13.040
Craig Torres from Bloomberg.

20:13.040 --> 20:17.570
I'd like to push for a little deeper explanation on thresholds and triggers.

20:17.570 --> 20:18.260
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

20:18.260 --> 20:19.100
Sure. CRAIG TORRES.

20:19.100 --> 20:22.430
The forecast and the mysterious dots kind of don't map

20:22.430 --> 20:24.770
into the unemployment forecast.

20:24.770 --> 20:28.500
We see more-gradual rate rise going out into time.

20:28.500 --> 20:31.460
People moving to the right, at least one person,

20:31.460 --> 20:34.860
on when they expect the rate to increase, and yet,

20:34.860 --> 20:39.310
unemployment's going to fall to 6.5 percent in 2014.

20:39.310 --> 20:44.660
I also note that labor force participation isn't in that great shape, and you,

20:44.660 --> 20:48.710
in fact, have been a big believer that a lot of the exit

20:48.710 --> 20:53.730
from the workforce is related to weak demand, not structural factors.

20:53.730 --> 20:55.590
So here is my question.

20:55.590 --> 21:03.950
Can you explain a little bit more-you know-maybe is the threshold too high?

21:03.950 --> 21:07.930
And I'll point out that the Vice Chair and two other people who used

21:07.930 --> 21:11.680
to work here have done significant research on,

21:11.680 --> 21:15.570
maybe you need to let the employment rate fall much lower

21:15.570 --> 21:17.780
to pull these people back into the labor force.

21:17.780 --> 21:19.100
So I'm wondering if you can expand on that.

21:19.100 --> 21:20.100
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

21:20.100 --> 21:23.570
Well, it's a great question and-but the-what you pointed out,

21:23.570 --> 21:25.350
the difference between the little dots

21:25.350 --> 21:29.040
and the forecast actually illustrates the point, which is, remember,

21:29.040 --> 21:32.870
the 6½ percent is a threshold, not a trigger.

21:32.870 --> 21:38.270
In other words, when we get to that point, we will then at that point begin to,

21:38.270 --> 21:42.910
you know, look at whether an increase in rates is appropriate,

21:42.910 --> 21:46.490
and among the things we would take into account, first of all, is inflation,

21:46.490 --> 21:50.550
and inflation obviously is very low and expected to stay low.

21:50.550 --> 21:53.360
Secondly, we would be taking into account,

21:53.360 --> 21:55.340
does that unemployment rate fairly represent

21:55.340 --> 21:57.060
in some sense the state of the labor market?

21:57.060 --> 22:01.180
And, as you pointed out, we have underemployment, part-time work,

22:01.180 --> 22:06.630
people leaving the labor force, reduced participation, long-term unemployment,

22:06.630 --> 22:07.890
a number of factors which suggest

22:07.890 --> 22:12.130
that maybe the 6.5 percent is a little bit-not exactly representative

22:12.130 --> 22:14.260
of the state of the labor market at that point.

22:14.260 --> 22:17.570
So, first of all, since it is a threshold and not a trigger,

22:17.570 --> 22:22.850
we're entirely free to take all that into account before we begin the process

22:22.850 --> 22:25.360
of raising rates, and that's what the diagram suggests.

22:25.360 --> 22:32.370
People are saying that unemployment will be at 6.5 in late 2014 or early 2015,

22:32.370 --> 22:35.050
but they're saying that increases in rates may not follow

22:35.050 --> 22:37.870
but several quarters after that.

22:37.870 --> 22:42.690
In terms of adjusting the threshold, I think that's something that might happen.

22:42.690 --> 22:48.760
If it did happen, it would be to lower it, I'm sure, not to raise it.

22:49.810 --> 22:52.540
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

22:52.540 --> 22:54.050
Binya Appelbaum, New York Times.

22:54.050 --> 22:56.930
Following up on that, so I understand the 6.5 percent is a threshold,

22:56.930 --> 23:00.980
but you've just talked about a 7 percent line for asset purchases.

23:00.980 --> 23:03.120
That sounded less like a threshold than like a target.

23:03.120 --> 23:04.750
You said if you're at about that level,

23:04.750 --> 23:07.490
you'll stop with the asset purchases mid next year.

23:07.490 --> 23:09.810
You talked about wanting to see substantial improvement

23:09.810 --> 23:12.570
in the labor market before you did suspend those purchases.

23:12.570 --> 23:15.250
It's very easy to imagine a situation in which we get to 7 percent

23:15.250 --> 23:17.780
without seeing labor force participation increase.

23:17.780 --> 23:20.580
The employment rate's been at the same level for three years now.

23:20.580 --> 23:23.890
Has something changed in your thinking about the value of asset purchases?

23:23.890 --> 23:26.890
Why are you cutting them off before you see that "substantial improvement"?

23:26.890 --> 23:27.680
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

23:27.680 --> 23:29.920
Well, "substantial" is in the eye of the beholder.

23:29.920 --> 23:39.000
I think going from 8.1 percent and a stagnant rate of improvement to 7 percent

23:39.000 --> 23:41.730
and stronger economic growth is a substantial increase.

23:41.730 --> 23:43.930
I think it's important to explain that, you know,

23:43.930 --> 23:45.660
we view ourselves as having two tools.

23:45.660 --> 23:48.540
One of them is rate policy, and that includes both setting the rate

23:48.540 --> 23:50.320
and providing guidance about future rates.

23:50.320 --> 23:52.740
That's our basic tool, that's the one that the Federal Reserve

23:52.740 --> 23:55.030
and other central banks have used forever.

23:55.030 --> 23:58.430
Asset purchases are a different kind of thing.

23:58.430 --> 24:01.510
They're unconventional policy, they come with certain risks

24:01.510 --> 24:07.960
and certain uncertainties that are not necessarily associated with rate policy.

24:07.960 --> 24:10.470
So our intent from the beginning, as I've been very clear,

24:10.470 --> 24:14.710
was to use asset purchases as a way of achieving some near-term momentum

24:14.710 --> 24:19.250
to get the economy moving forward into a sustainable recovery.

24:19.250 --> 24:24.770
And then, essentially, to allow the low interest rate policy which-to carry

24:24.770 --> 24:25.550
us through.

24:25.550 --> 24:28.390
So what I-so let me just make two, I think, very important points.

24:28.390 --> 24:32.470
The first is, our target is not 7, it's not 6½,

24:32.470 --> 24:36.890
our target is maximum employment, which, according to our projections,

24:36.890 --> 24:39.380
most people on the Committee think is somewhere between 5

24:39.380 --> 24:42.200
and 6 percent unemployment, and that's where we're trying to get to.

24:42.200 --> 24:45.450
The 7, the 6½-these are guide posts that tell you how we're going

24:45.450 --> 24:51.440
to be shifting the mix of our tools as we try to land this ship on a, you know,

24:51.440 --> 24:59.150
on a-in a smooth way onto the aircraft carrier.

24:59.150 --> 25:00.850
The-sorry.

25:00.850 --> 25:06.610
So, the other thing I wanted to say was that stopping asset purchases,

25:06.610 --> 25:10.900
when that happens, and I think we're still some distance from that happening,

25:10.900 --> 25:16.230
but when that happens, that won't involve ending the stimulus

25:16.230 --> 25:19.910
from asset purchases because we're going to hold on to that portfolio.

25:19.910 --> 25:24.570
And if the stock theory of the portfolio is correct, which we believe it is,

25:24.570 --> 25:29.380
holding all of those securities off of the market and reinvesting

25:29.380 --> 25:33.950
and still keeping the, you know, rolling-over maturing securities,

25:33.950 --> 25:38.900
will still continue to put downward pressure on interest rates.

25:38.900 --> 25:42.750
And so, between our commitments to a low federal funds rate

25:42.750 --> 25:46.430
and the large portfolio, we will still be producing a very large amount

25:46.430 --> 25:48.260
of stimulus-in our view, enough

25:48.260 --> 25:52.700
to bring the economy smoothly towards full employment

25:52.700 --> 25:57.760
without incurring unnecessary costs or risks.

26:00.600 --> 26:02.980
VICTORIA McGRANE.

26:02.980 --> 26:03.440
Hi, Chairman.

26:03.440 --> 26:05.760
Victoria McGrane with Dow Jones Newswires.

26:05.760 --> 26:09.430
You, in your statement before the press conference and in the policy statement,

26:09.430 --> 26:12.520
acknowledge that inflation readings have been low.

26:12.520 --> 26:16.940
But you maintain that inflation expect-longer-term inflation expectations have

26:16.940 --> 26:17.870
remained stable.

26:17.870 --> 26:20.340
But actually, certain bond market measures

26:20.340 --> 26:23.610
of these things have fallen in recent weeks.

26:23.610 --> 26:26.320
Is that of any concern, and if not, why?

26:26.320 --> 26:30.770
What would you need to see for the Committee to start being more concerned

26:30.770 --> 26:34.110
that longer-term inflation expectations are, in fact, falling?

26:34.110 --> 26:35.560
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

26:35.560 --> 26:37.350
Well, this is something we watch very carefully.

26:37.350 --> 26:40.380
There are a number of, as I mentioned in the statement,

26:40.380 --> 26:44.070
there are a number of transitory factors that may be contributing

26:44.070 --> 26:46.340
to the very low inflation rate.

26:46.340 --> 26:50.100
For example, the effects of the sequester on medical payments,

26:50.100 --> 26:55.120
the fact that nonmarket prices are extraordinarily low right now.

26:55.120 --> 26:58.240
So these are some things that we expect to reverse and we expect

26:58.240 --> 27:00.580
to see inflation come up a bit.

27:00.580 --> 27:04.230
But, first, on inflation expectations,

27:04.230 --> 27:05.880
it is true that the breakevens

27:05.880 --> 27:10.950
from the inflation-adjusted-inflation-indexed bonds have come down.

27:10.950 --> 27:14.010
To this point, they still remain within the historical range

27:14.010 --> 27:16.630
that we've seen over the past few years.

27:16.630 --> 27:20.010
And moreover, other measures of inflation expectations,

27:20.010 --> 27:23.410
be it forecasts by professional forecasters,

27:23.410 --> 27:28.050
whether it's survey measures from firms or households,

27:28.050 --> 27:33.230
those are all still pretty much in the same places they were.

27:33.230 --> 27:36.460
Now that being said, and as I said in my opening remarks,

27:36.460 --> 27:38.830
we don't take anything for granted.

27:38.830 --> 27:43.740
And one of the preconditions for the policy path that I described is

27:43.740 --> 27:45.790
that inflation begin, at least gradually,

27:45.790 --> 27:49.690
to return towards our 2 percent objective.

27:49.690 --> 27:52.260
If that doesn't happen, we will obviously have

27:52.260 --> 27:54.120
to take some measures to address that.

27:54.120 --> 28:01.260
And we are certainly determined to keep inflation not only-we want

28:01.260 --> 28:02.900
to keep inflation near its objective,

28:02.900 --> 28:05.630
not only avoiding inflation that's too high,

28:05.630 --> 28:08.080
but we also want to avoid inflation that's too low.

28:08.080 --> 28:14.820
PETER COOK.

28:14.820 --> 28:17.570
Mr. Chairman, Peter Cook with Bloomberg Television.

28:17.570 --> 28:21.180
Given the volatility we've seen in the market since your comments of May 22nd,

28:21.180 --> 28:25.420
the lengths to which you are stressing the forward guidance here today,

28:25.420 --> 28:26.990
it does suggest that there are some

28:26.990 --> 28:29.770
out there you're worried are not getting your message right now.

28:29.770 --> 28:32.440
And I'm wondering, to what extent do you think now

28:32.440 --> 28:36.120
that your exit strategy is going to be that much more challenging because even

28:36.120 --> 28:39.170
in this small period of time when you haven't done all that much,

28:39.170 --> 28:40.610
you've seen this kind of reaction?

28:40.610 --> 28:42.000
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

28:42.000 --> 28:45.390
Well, it is important for us to communicate.

28:45.390 --> 28:51.180
It's particularly important when we have an unusual economic situation

28:51.180 --> 28:55.870
where I think the standard relationships are not applying in the way they have,

28:55.870 --> 28:59.880
where we're using unconventional tools, where we're using forward guidance.

28:59.880 --> 29:01.000
So, I guess I agree with you

29:01.000 --> 29:04.970
that our communication is going to be very important.

29:04.970 --> 29:11.130
We hope that the-again, the key point I've tried to make today is

29:11.130 --> 29:15.370
that our policies are tied to how the outlook evolves.

29:15.370 --> 29:20.490
And that should provide some comfort to markets because they will understand,

29:20.490 --> 29:26.770
I hope, that we will be providing whatever support is necessary.

29:26.770 --> 29:30.350
If the economy does not improve along the lines that we expect,

29:30.350 --> 29:32.500
we'll provide additional support.

29:32.500 --> 29:35.070
If financial conditions evolve in a way that's inconsistent

29:35.070 --> 29:37.620
with economic recovery, we will provide support.

29:37.620 --> 29:38.840
But we're hope-and in that way,

29:38.840 --> 29:42.080
we hope to increase confidence both among market participants,

29:42.080 --> 29:50.090
but also among investors and private consumers and other people in the economy.

29:50.090 --> 29:54.560
So, but again, I mean, your point is well taken that we are in a position

29:54.560 --> 29:59.350
where the simple adjustment by 25 basis points to the federal funds rate seems

29:59.350 --> 30:06.060
like a long-ago experience, and we are in a more complex type of situation.

30:06.060 --> 30:11.470
But we are determined to be as clear as we can, and we hope that you

30:11.470 --> 30:17.080
and your listeners and the markets will all be able to follow what we're saying.

30:18.210 --> 30:20.260
DONNA BORAK.

30:21.330 --> 30:23.820
Donna Borak with American Banker.

30:23.820 --> 30:27.480
Next month will be the three-year anniversary of the Dodd-Frank Act, and,

30:27.480 --> 30:30.430
as you know, there are a number of significant rulemakings that have left

30:30.430 --> 30:34.780
to be finalized: the Volcker rule, prudential regulations under section 165

30:34.780 --> 30:38.700
and 166, and risk retention, to name a few.

30:38.700 --> 30:43.040
Can you provide us with an update on where we stand with the rulemakings?

30:43.040 --> 30:44.510
And also, are you still optimistic

30:44.510 --> 30:47.560
that we will see these rules completed by the end of this year?

30:47.560 --> 30:49.470
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

30:49.470 --> 30:54.220
It's certainly true that it's taken time to do these regulations.

30:54.220 --> 30:55.430
There are a number of reasons for that.

30:55.430 --> 31:00.440
The first is that they are, inherently, many of them, quite complicated.

31:00.440 --> 31:03.320
The Volcker rule, for example, involves some very subtle distinctions

31:03.320 --> 31:06.980
between hedging and market making and proprietary trading.

31:06.980 --> 31:12.050
The second reason is that many of them involve multiple agencies,

31:12.050 --> 31:15.880
which have to coordinate, cooperate, and agree on language.

31:15.880 --> 31:21.870
I think the QRM rule is, six agencies are involved in making that rule.

31:21.870 --> 31:24.580
And the third fact-the third and basic issue is

31:24.580 --> 31:25.940
that we really have to do our homework.

31:25.940 --> 31:29.950
We have to get these right, and that means having extended comment periods,

31:29.950 --> 31:32.190
getting lots of information from the public.

31:32.190 --> 31:35.040
And then, you know, reviewing those comments and doing all we can

31:35.040 --> 31:39.830
to make sure we're responsive to those many, many concerns and suggestions.

31:39.830 --> 31:41.250
So it does take time.

31:41.250 --> 31:43.910
I think it's a little unfair to say, "Well,

31:43.910 --> 31:48.210
only 30 percent of the rules have been completed," because most of the rules,

31:48.210 --> 31:50.490
even if they haven't been completed, are now very far advanced,

31:50.490 --> 31:51.950
and that's true for the major rules.

31:51.950 --> 31:57.420
We are very close, for example, to completing Basel III.

31:57.420 --> 32:01.020
We have made a good bit of progress on the Volcker rule,

32:01.020 --> 32:04.580
and I do anticipate that being done this year.

32:04.580 --> 32:08.090
We are making additional progress on our 165,

32:08.090 --> 32:11.860
166 advisory rules on the capital surcharges.

32:11.860 --> 32:17.180
These are all things that will be coming relatively soon, at least during,

32:17.180 --> 32:18.580
you know, the current year.

32:18.580 --> 32:24.790
And of course, once they are out there, then it will still take some time

32:24.790 --> 32:27.670
to be implemented, for financial institutions

32:27.670 --> 32:32.990
to change their practices, and so on-and so on.

32:32.990 --> 32:36.790
I would also emphasize, though, that, even as this is going on,

32:36.790 --> 32:41.020
that we are not ignoring the health and safety of the banking system.

32:41.020 --> 32:43.560
For example, I mean, since 2009,

32:43.560 --> 32:46.660
we've been doing these very rigorous stress tests,

32:46.660 --> 32:48.900
which are part of the Dodd-Frank rules.

32:48.900 --> 32:54.050
And the amount of capital that U.S. banks hold has roughly doubled-the largest

32:54.050 --> 32:56.630
banks-since 2009.

32:56.630 --> 32:59.520
And indeed, the largest banks now appear, most of them,

32:59.520 --> 33:04.160
either to be Basel III-compliant or pretty close to Basel III-compliant.

33:04.160 --> 33:07.580
So we are working with the banks to ensure their safety,

33:07.580 --> 33:10.130
to help them move in the directions that they know they're going to have

33:10.130 --> 33:14.640
to be going even as the rules themselves are being finalized.

33:14.640 --> 33:21.210
So, it's an ongoing process, but I expect to see, you know,

33:21.210 --> 33:26.490
more-rapid completion, you know, going forward in the next few quarters.

33:26.490 --> 33:29.760
PETER BARNES.

33:29.760 --> 33:31.600
Peter Barnes, Fox Business.

33:31.600 --> 33:35.060
Sir, one of the highlights of the Kansas City Fed conference

33:35.060 --> 33:38.630
in Jackson Hole every year are remarks by the Chairman, you.

33:38.630 --> 33:40.550
You're not going this year.

33:40.550 --> 33:44.710
We've heard it's because you have a conflict in your personal schedule.

33:44.710 --> 33:50.890
But some have read-taken that as a sign that you may not be staying

33:50.890 --> 33:53.410
on the job for another term.

33:53.410 --> 33:55.130
Could you comment on that?

33:55.130 --> 33:57.420
And could you offer us-give us a little more explanation

33:57.420 --> 34:00.250
as to why you're not going to be in Jackson Hole for the first time?

34:00.250 --> 34:00.850
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

34:00.850 --> 34:02.280
Well, as I've said, I'm not going to comment

34:02.280 --> 34:04.890
on my personal plans, but I will say this.

34:04.890 --> 34:06.570
I think there's a perception

34:06.570 --> 34:11.230
that the Jackson Hole conference is a Federal Reserve Systemwide conference.

34:11.230 --> 34:15.010
It's not. It's a conference sponsored by one of the 12 Reserve Banks.

34:15.010 --> 34:18.840
Every one of the 12 Reserve Banks has conferences, has meetings,

34:18.840 --> 34:22.840
and this is the one I've gone to the most of probably any of the Reserve Banks,

34:22.840 --> 34:26.870
so I think it's not inappropriate to go to different conferences,

34:26.870 --> 34:31.630
different meetings, and to essentially meet all the constituents that I have

34:31.630 --> 34:33.010
in these different Reserve Banks.

34:33.010 --> 34:36.740
So that's one reason, certainly.

34:36.740 --> 34:38.030
STEVE BECKNER.

34:38.030 --> 34:43.490
Steve Beckner of MNI, Mr. Chairman.

34:43.490 --> 34:49.580
A number of your colleagues, as reflected in the new SEP projections,

34:49.580 --> 34:54.950
expect the unemployment rate to get down to 6½ percent next year,

34:54.950 --> 35:00.080
which is your threshold for considering raising the funds rate.

35:00.080 --> 35:07.210
And yet the FOMC has also said that it expects to keep rates very accommodative

35:07.210 --> 35:10.740
for a considerable time after asset purchases

35:10.740 --> 35:13.370
and after the recovery has strengthened.

35:13.370 --> 35:17.610
And yet here we are, the middle of 2013,

35:17.610 --> 35:23.070
you've not even begun to scale back asset purchases.

35:23.070 --> 35:25.160
So, I mean, you've partially addressed this.

35:25.160 --> 35:28.780
But, you know, could there be a conflict between, on the one hand,

35:28.780 --> 35:34.820
the asset purchase program, on the other hand, the funds rate guidance policy.

35:34.820 --> 35:37.020
Could they conflict?

35:37.020 --> 35:38.560
Could you perhaps elaborate?

35:38.560 --> 35:38.950
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

35:38.950 --> 35:41.360
Well, I certainly hope the unemployment rate comes down so fast

35:41.360 --> 35:43.430
that this becomes a problem.

35:43.430 --> 35:45.250
I guess I would point out a couple of things.

35:45.250 --> 35:51.300
One is that, of course, you know, there's a range of estimates,

35:51.300 --> 35:55.090
and they're all based on each individual's idea of optimal policy,

35:55.090 --> 35:57.750
so the policy assumptions may not be the same.

35:57.750 --> 36:03.410
So it's true that some are as low as 6.5, but as I said in my earlier answer,

36:03.410 --> 36:05.320
that's a threshold, not a trigger.

36:05.320 --> 36:10.070
So, evidently, if you, you know, look at the policy expectations that are given

36:10.070 --> 36:14.420
in the dot diagram, you'll see that the very strong majority

36:14.420 --> 36:21.670
of FOMC participants still expect rates to be quite low at the end of 2015.

36:21.670 --> 36:25.350
So that's not inconsistent, that's just saying that people are looking

36:25.350 --> 36:28.100
at a variety of factors, including inflation,

36:28.100 --> 36:33.090
which is predicted to be quite low, and other perhaps labor market factors

36:33.090 --> 36:38.350
in thinking about when it'll be appropriate to start increasing rates.

36:38.350 --> 36:39.750
SCOTT SPOERRY.

36:39.750 --> 36:44.430
Thank you, Sir.

36:44.430 --> 36:47.250
Scott Spoerry from CNN, CNNMoney.

36:47.250 --> 36:51.950
Well, first of all, I should tell you that your analogy to landing the economy

36:51.950 --> 36:54.650
on an aircraft carrier worried me a little bit because,

36:54.650 --> 36:58.580
from personal experience, I find that it's always a little bit jarring

36:58.580 --> 37:00.700
to land on an aircraft carrier.

37:00.700 --> 37:03.540
But I wanted to talk about mortgage-backed securities.

37:03.540 --> 37:08.990
You mentioned during your comments here that, if I understood correctly,

37:08.990 --> 37:12.700
that you're not going to dispose of the mortgage-backed securities that you have

37:12.700 --> 37:16.090
on the book during this period of normalization.

37:16.090 --> 37:20.150
And I've heard many people on Wall Street and elsewhere say

37:20.150 --> 37:26.780
that right now the Federal Reserve is the market for mortgage-backed securities,

37:26.780 --> 37:31.550
and so-which means that it's kind of a warped market right now.

37:31.550 --> 37:37.220
And I'm just wondering, how focused are you on mortgage-backed securities

37:37.220 --> 37:41.470
and this larger world market, which-what used to be a world market

37:41.470 --> 37:43.470
for mortgage-backed securities.

37:43.470 --> 37:48.040
And I guess-I guess what I'm saying, has the ground shifted from under us

37:48.040 --> 37:52.980
in terms of the mortgage-backed security world on a permanent basis,

37:52.980 --> 37:57.300
or at least for a very long-term basis, because of the devastating nature

37:57.300 --> 37:58.760
of what happened a few years ago?

37:58.760 --> 38:01.150
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

38:01.150 --> 38:06.460
Well, we are still only a fraction of the total holdings

38:06.460 --> 38:12.820
of mortgage-backed securities, but, more relevant,

38:12.820 --> 38:17.630
as part of our assessment-of our ongoing assessment of the potential costs

38:17.630 --> 38:19.830
of our various asset purchase programs,

38:19.830 --> 38:22.430
we pay very close attention to market functioning.

38:22.430 --> 38:26.670
And our assessment is that the MBS market is still quite a healthy market

38:26.670 --> 38:30.000
in terms of the spreads, in terms of execution times,

38:30.000 --> 38:32.600
in terms of the number of people on both sides of the market.

38:32.600 --> 38:38.700
You know, there are REITs now that are building up their MBS portfolios.

38:38.700 --> 38:41.380
There are plenty of real-money investors holding MBS.

38:41.380 --> 38:47.960
So, you know, if the market was really breaking down in some way

38:47.960 --> 38:49.800
that would be a factor we'd have to take into account.

38:49.800 --> 38:52.730
But our assessment-and, of course, we're in that market quite a bit,

38:52.730 --> 38:56.230
so we have a lot of information about it-our assessment is

38:56.230 --> 38:59.230
that that market is still working quite well,

38:59.230 --> 39:05.340
and that our purchases are not disrupting the normal price discovery

39:05.340 --> 39:09.080
and liquidity functions of that market.

39:09.080 --> 39:13.950
I think that the events of five years ago, obviously,

39:13.950 --> 39:15.370
do have a big long-term effect.

39:15.370 --> 39:21.950
There are bills in Congress that would change, reform the GSEs, for example,

39:21.950 --> 39:26.410
and ultimately would change the market for mortgage-backed securities,

39:26.410 --> 39:29.790
perhaps increasing the amount of private placements

39:29.790 --> 39:32.310
or changing the whole institutional structure of that market.

39:32.310 --> 39:36.310
So, we may end up holding some securities,

39:36.310 --> 39:43.470
which are in some sense left over from a previous era at some point.

39:43.470 --> 39:47.040
But nevertheless, you know, for the time being,

39:47.040 --> 39:49.600
they are the mortgage-backed securities market- Fannie

39:49.600 --> 39:53.560
and Freddie are basically it-and we are, of course,

39:53.560 --> 39:56.010
legally allowed to buy and own those securities.

39:56.010 --> 40:00.160
And so, we have found it useful to do that, and we believe it's contributed

40:00.160 --> 40:02.290
to lower mortgage rates and a stronger housing market.

40:02.290 --> 40:04.520
So that's been our rationale.

40:04.520 --> 40:06.960
And just, again, to come back to your question,

40:06.960 --> 40:11.680
we do not see any significant deterioration in market functioning.

40:11.680 --> 40:13.150
If there are things that you can point us to,

40:13.150 --> 40:14.810
we would be obviously very interested.

40:14.810 --> 40:15.250
SCOTT SPOERRY.

40:15.250 --> 40:21.580
Well, let me just follow up with just one thing.

40:21.580 --> 40:24.950
In terms of the government backing through the GSEs

40:24.950 --> 40:28.080
of mortgage-backed securities, is there a concern,

40:28.080 --> 40:35.180
a legitimate concern that without government backing of this market in some way,

40:35.180 --> 40:40.670
that because the private market is-seems to move with much more rapidity

40:40.670 --> 40:44.860
than government backing and government purchases,

40:44.860 --> 40:49.920
that moving the government out of this backup role

40:49.920 --> 40:53.450
on mortgage-backed securities would be a real problem

40:53.450 --> 40:57.950
or really change the nature of getting a mortgage in America.

40:57.950 --> 41:01.580
Some people say, you know, if the government wasn't behind it,

41:01.580 --> 41:04.340
it would mean the end of the 30-year mortgage as we know it.

41:04.340 --> 41:05.830
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

41:05.830 --> 41:07.140
Well, these issues are being debated.

41:07.140 --> 41:12.800
There are some-a number of bills in Congress which would change the GSEs,

41:12.800 --> 41:14.460
would eliminate them in some cases,

41:14.460 --> 41:17.350
or would place them with backstop government support,

41:17.350 --> 41:20.990
as opposed to 100 percent government credit guarantees.

41:20.990 --> 41:24.610
So these are debates that we're all having about the future

41:24.610 --> 41:26.690
of the U.S. mortgage market.

41:26.690 --> 41:30.030
I think it's entirely possible that if there's major change

41:30.030 --> 41:33.860
in the government's role in the mortgage market

41:33.860 --> 41:37.190
that we might see a different structure in mortgages.

41:37.190 --> 41:42.080
And, you know, other countries have different structures, and they have,

41:42.080 --> 41:46.790
in many cases, the same or similar home ownership rates as we do,

41:46.790 --> 41:48.800
so it's possible that we may find

41:48.800 --> 41:52.650
that a different structure is better for some people.

41:52.650 --> 41:54.840
GREG ROBB.

41:56.970 --> 41:58.180
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

41:58.180 --> 41:59.830
Greg Robb, MarketWatch.com.

41:59.830 --> 42:03.800
I was wondering if you could go back over your-what you said about the plans

42:03.800 --> 42:05.770
for tapering later this year.

42:05.770 --> 42:09.320
And why isn't tapering a tightening?

42:09.320 --> 42:12.570
It seems that many people in the markets just as soon

42:12.570 --> 42:17.090
as you talk taper-will taper-are just going to push forward when you-they think

42:17.090 --> 42:18.890
that first rate hike will come.

42:18.890 --> 42:20.040
Thank you.

42:20.040 --> 42:20.670
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

42:20.670 --> 42:26.570
Well, as I tried to explain in my opening remarks,

42:26.570 --> 42:31.320
our plans depend on the economic scenario and how it evolves.

42:31.320 --> 42:38.660
And what I tried to do is explain how our asset purchases would evolve if,

42:38.660 --> 42:43.660
sort of, the modal, the most likely forecast, were to, in fact, take place,

42:43.660 --> 42:45.380
which, of course, it won't exactly take place,

42:45.380 --> 42:47.330
something else will no doubt happen.

42:47.330 --> 42:51.040
But again, our basic forecast is one which is basically,

42:51.040 --> 42:55.850
as was pointed out earlier by Mr. Hilsenrath, a moderately optimistic forecast,

42:55.850 --> 43:00.130
where growth picks up as we pass through this period of fiscal restraint;

43:00.130 --> 43:03.800
where unemployment continues to fall at a gradual pace as it has been

43:03.800 --> 43:07.810
since last September-and we have made some progress since last September;

43:07.810 --> 43:11.330
and inflation rises slowly towards 2 percent.

43:11.330 --> 43:15.310
Those are the conditions that define this sort of baseline forecast.

43:15.310 --> 43:22.190
In that case then, as I described it, we would expect probably to slow

43:22.190 --> 43:24.620
or moderate purchases some time later this year,

43:24.620 --> 43:30.050
and then through the middle of-through the early part of next year, and ending,

43:30.050 --> 43:32.730
in that scenario, somewhere in the middle of the year.

43:32.730 --> 43:36.600
Again though, it's very important to understand that that's-if we do that,

43:36.600 --> 43:38.390
that would basically say that, you know,

43:38.390 --> 43:43.150
that we've had a relatively decent economic outcome in terms

43:43.150 --> 43:46.650
of sustained improvement in growth and unemployment.

43:46.650 --> 43:48.570
If things are worse, we would do more.

43:48.570 --> 43:50.650
If things are better, we will do less.

43:50.650 --> 43:54.820
I think I would-to answer your other question-I would draw the analogy:

43:54.820 --> 43:59.980
If the Federal Reserve in normal times lowers the federal funds rate

43:59.980 --> 44:06.780
by 25 basis points, but some traders think that-were expecting 50 basis points,

44:06.780 --> 44:09.830
then there might be a sense that the financial conditions have

44:09.830 --> 44:11.040
tightened somewhat.

44:11.040 --> 44:13.920
But nevertheless, I think you would say that if the Fed cut the funds rate

44:13.920 --> 44:17.720
by 25 basis points, that that was an easing of policy.

44:17.720 --> 44:20.340
And, by the same token, as long as we're buying assets,

44:20.340 --> 44:22.960
we are adding to our holdings.

44:22.960 --> 44:26.280
We do believe-although, you know, there's room for debate-we do believe

44:26.280 --> 44:32.200
that the primary effect of our purchases is through the stock that we hold,

44:32.200 --> 44:35.210
because that stock has been withdrawn from markets,

44:35.210 --> 44:40.590
and the prices of those assets have to adjust to balance supply and demand.

44:40.590 --> 44:42.060
And we've taken out some of the supply,

44:42.060 --> 44:45.140
and so the prices go up, the yields go down.

44:45.140 --> 44:49.390
So, that, seems to me, consistent with the idea

44:49.390 --> 44:51.150
that we're still adding liquidity,

44:51.150 --> 44:56.700
we're still adding accommodation to the system.

44:56.700 --> 44:57.640
RYAN AVENT.

44:57.640 --> 45:02.350
Ryan Avent, The Economist.

45:02.350 --> 45:03.620
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

45:03.620 --> 45:04.740
Hi. RYAN AVENT.

45:04.740 --> 45:08.700
Mr. Chairman, I'm trying to sort of understand the view with relation

45:08.700 --> 45:09.800
to these inflation figures.

45:09.800 --> 45:14.150
I'm a little surprised at how, I guess, blasé the Committee seems about them,

45:14.150 --> 45:17.070
with the exception of President Bullard.

45:17.070 --> 45:23.390
Inflation looks remarkably low on both core and headline, PCE and CPI.

45:23.390 --> 45:27.300
Your projections have it at-rising, the core PCE rising,

45:27.300 --> 45:30.790
to at most 2 percent in 2015.

45:30.790 --> 45:34.220
You say that inflation expectations have remained sort of within the range

45:34.220 --> 45:36.110
that the Fed has traditionally been comfortable with,

45:36.110 --> 45:39.280
but they have fallen by a good ½ percentage point.

45:39.280 --> 45:43.370
And, as you know, that when interest rates are stuck at the zero lower bound,

45:43.370 --> 45:46.230
a decline in inflation expectations sort of translates

45:46.230 --> 45:48.880
into an increase in real rates.

45:48.880 --> 45:50.860
Why is the Fed not more concerned about this?

45:50.860 --> 45:52.260
It seems to me that earlier in the recovery,

45:52.260 --> 45:55.570
they were more concerned about declines in inflation like this.

45:55.570 --> 45:58.780
And wouldn't you say that, even if you're happy with the pace

45:58.780 --> 46:01.270
of labor market recovery, that, other things equal,

46:01.270 --> 46:02.810
this sort of inflation performance suggests

46:02.810 --> 46:04.650
that you should be pushing harder on the accelerator.

46:04.650 --> 46:06.370
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

46:06.370 --> 46:08.080
I don't disagree with anything you said.

46:08.080 --> 46:11.370
I think low-inflation that's too low is a problem.

46:11.370 --> 46:13.030
It increases the risk of deflation.

46:13.030 --> 46:14.940
It raises real interest rates.

46:14.940 --> 46:18.610
It means that debt deleveraging takes place more slowly.

46:18.610 --> 46:23.960
Now, there's always issues about, you know, why is it low?

46:23.960 --> 46:28.270
And, as I pointed out, there are a few reasons that are probably not

46:28.270 --> 46:30.170
that meaningful economically-for example,

46:30.170 --> 46:34.050
the temporary movement in medical prices,

46:34.050 --> 46:37.980
the temporary movements in nonmarket prices, things of that sort.

46:37.980 --> 46:40.550
I mean, after all, the CPI is somewhat higher.

46:40.550 --> 46:44.140
And so we expect inflation to come back up, that's our forecast.

46:44.140 --> 46:48.460
But I don't want-I think it's entirely wrong

46:48.460 --> 46:49.610
to say we're not concerned about it.

46:49.610 --> 46:50.990
We are concerned about it.

46:50.990 --> 46:55.010
We would like to get inflation up to our target,

46:55.010 --> 46:59.500
and that will be a factor in our thinking about the thresholds.

46:59.500 --> 47:02.500
It will be a factor in our thinking about asset purchases.

47:02.500 --> 47:05.990
And, you know, we've got a dual mandate,

47:05.990 --> 47:08.810
and it's maximum employment and price stability.

47:08.810 --> 47:13.010
And there's a reason why we define price stability as a positive inflation rate,

47:13.010 --> 47:17.590
not zero, because we believe that in order to best maximize the mandate,

47:17.590 --> 47:21.030
we need to have enough inflation so that there is, in fact, you know,

47:21.030 --> 47:23.350
some room for real interest rates to move.

47:23.350 --> 47:27.710
So, I don't disagree with your basic argument.

47:27.710 --> 47:29.060
KEVIN HALL.

47:33.930 --> 47:38.790
Thanks, Mr. Chairman, Kevin Hall with McClatchy Newspapers.

47:38.790 --> 47:42.250
Since you've referred to Mr. Hilsenrath, is he the real power behind the throne?

47:42.250 --> 47:46.030
That's one of the questions we all have.

47:46.030 --> 47:51.750
You had mentioned on several occasions now that quantitative easing is designed

47:51.750 --> 47:53.940
to kind of spur economic-drive down the yields,

47:53.940 --> 47:56.930
force more risk-taking that flows through the economy.

47:56.930 --> 47:59.730
There had been debate a couple of years ago at the beginning of this

47:59.730 --> 48:01.910
about whether it was inflating commodity prices.

48:01.910 --> 48:03.700
Inflation as a whole is subdued,

48:03.700 --> 48:09.600
but oil prices are-anchored somewhere around $99-$93 to $99.

48:09.600 --> 48:13.120
We were told once we have domestic production-we have record production now-that

48:13.120 --> 48:14.930
that would be a signal to bring prices down.

48:14.930 --> 48:15.900
It hasn't happened.

48:15.900 --> 48:18.720
Brazil has got people on the streets because of inflation.

48:18.720 --> 48:24.140
To what degree do you think quantitative easing is actually inflating commodity

48:24.140 --> 48:26.690
prices, and have you been able to filter that out?

48:26.690 --> 48:30.160
And any thoughts on wage growth and why that's been so flat

48:30.160 --> 48:32.780
when everything else seems to be doing good in the economy?

48:32.780 --> 48:33.360
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

48:33.360 --> 48:38.010
Well, when we-as I recall, I believe I have this right-when we introduced the

48:38.010 --> 48:44.820
second round of LSAPs, properly known as QE2, in, I think, November 2010,

48:44.820 --> 48:48.280
there was a lot of increase in commodity prices at that time,

48:48.280 --> 48:53.650
and there was a lot of complaining that the Fed is pumping up commodity prices,

48:53.650 --> 48:58.220
and that that's a negative for people around the world.

48:58.220 --> 49:03.780
And we argued at the time that the effects of the Federal Reserve's policy

49:03.780 --> 49:06.730
on global commodity prices was probably pretty small,

49:06.730 --> 49:09.680
and that it operated-to the extent it did have an effect-it operated

49:09.680 --> 49:12.340
through growth-mostly through growth expectations.

49:12.340 --> 49:17.980
That is, a stronger global economy tends to drive up commodity prices.

49:17.980 --> 49:22.670
This time around, we've purchased, and are in the process of purchasing,

49:22.670 --> 49:26.200
a lot more than we did in so-called QE2.

49:26.200 --> 49:31.510
We haven't really seen much increase in commodity prices.

49:31.510 --> 49:35.940
Commodity prices are way off their peaks of early last year.

49:35.940 --> 49:39.290
Oil is a little bit different from others in that it's kind of hung up.

49:39.290 --> 49:42.290
But many other commodity prices have fallen further,

49:42.290 --> 49:49.420
and the reason I would give for that is that the emerging markets-China,

49:49.420 --> 49:53.630
the rest of Asia, and some other parts of the world-plus Europe, of course,

49:53.630 --> 49:57.160
are softer, and so global commodity demand is weaker.

49:57.160 --> 49:59.040
And that explains, I think, the bulk

49:59.040 --> 50:01.310
of why commodity prices have not risen so much.

50:01.310 --> 50:07.290
So I think that's all consistent with our story that the effect

50:07.290 --> 50:11.070
of asset purchases on commodity prices-I'm not saying it's zero,

50:11.070 --> 50:17.330
but I don't think that it's nearly as big as some folks have suggested.

50:17.330 --> 50:21.950
In terms of wages, I think that's mostly consistent with our view

50:21.950 --> 50:25.590
that unemployment, at 7.6 percent,

50:25.590 --> 50:29.560
is still pretty far from where we should be satisfied.

50:29.560 --> 50:33.370
Maximum employment, we think, is, again, between 5 and 6 percent,

50:33.370 --> 50:36.500
although these are very difficult numbers to estimate.

50:36.500 --> 50:42.470
So, very weak wage growth, except in a few places and a few narrow occupations,

50:42.470 --> 50:48.200
is indicative to me of a labor market that remains quite slack and where,

50:48.200 --> 50:50.300
you know, that justifies, I think-together

50:50.300 --> 50:53.940
with low inflation-justifies why we are maintaining a highly

50:53.940 --> 50:56.750
accommodative policy.

50:56.750 --> 51:01.450
KATE DAVIDSON.

51:01.450 --> 51:03.690
Mr. Chairman, Kate Davidson from Politico.

51:03.690 --> 51:09.440
The SEC's money market fund proposal is far less comprehensive than the plan

51:09.440 --> 51:12.870
that you and the Financial Stability Oversight Council endorsed.

51:12.870 --> 51:17.930
Do you think FSOC should defer to the SEC or still press for more to be done?

51:17.930 --> 51:19.050
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

51:19.050 --> 51:27.040
Well, I'm very glad to see that the SEC has taken up money market reform.

51:27.040 --> 51:31.160
It is by far the best outcome for the SEC to do it.

51:31.160 --> 51:35.650
It's the area where they have the expertise and where they have the experience.

51:35.650 --> 51:38.600
In terms of their actual proposal they've put out-of course,

51:38.600 --> 51:44.350
it's just a proposal for comment-one of their two proposals, the floating NAV,

51:44.350 --> 51:50.270
the floating net asset value, is, of course, qualitatively similar to that-one

51:50.270 --> 51:58.170
of the proposals that was in the FSOC suggestions.

51:58.170 --> 52:05.760
We have not yet reviewed this in enough detail to give a view.

52:05.760 --> 52:10.030
But I hope that-I think-I know for sure that by putting

52:10.030 --> 52:13.490
out a floating NAV proposal that they're moving in the right direction.

52:13.490 --> 52:18.080
And I'm hopeful that what comes out will be something that's sufficient

52:18.080 --> 52:21.660
to meet the very important need of stabilizing the money market funds.

52:21.660 --> 52:24.080
AKIO FUJII.

52:27.680 --> 52:31.560
I'm Akio Fujii, Nikkei Newspaper.

52:31.560 --> 52:33.320
Thank you, Chairman.

52:33.320 --> 52:36.320
Recently, we have seen great volatility

52:36.320 --> 52:41.360
in Japanese market-equity, JGB, and foreign exchange.

52:41.360 --> 52:46.160
Some say this volatility is due to uncertainty

52:46.160 --> 52:49.300
to the Federal Reserve's monetary policy direction,

52:49.300 --> 52:53.320
but others say this is due to lack of confidence to the Bank

52:53.320 --> 52:55.240
of Japan's monetary policy.

52:55.240 --> 52:59.720
So how do you view the Bank of Japan's efforts?

52:59.720 --> 53:02.190
Do you still support Bank of Japan policy?

53:02.190 --> 53:08.100
And other question is, how much do you pay attention to the spillover effect

53:08.100 --> 53:13.790
to the international market when you consider exit strategy?

53:13.790 --> 53:15.870
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

53:15.870 --> 53:21.760
Well, I think the volatility is mostly linked to the Bank of Japan's efforts,

53:21.760 --> 53:25.780
and that would seem logical, since in earlier episodes,

53:25.780 --> 53:28.070
when the Fed was doing asset purchases

53:28.070 --> 53:31.610
and the BOJ was not doing anything, there was no volatility.

53:31.610 --> 53:37.200
So it sort of seems logical that the change here is the change in BOJ policy.

53:37.200 --> 53:42.270
The BOJ is fighting against a very difficult, entrenched deflation.

53:42.270 --> 53:45.580
And, of course, deflation has been a problem in Japan for many, many years,

53:45.580 --> 53:49.500
which means that expectations are very much-the public's expectations are

53:49.500 --> 53:53.530
for continuing deflation, and therefore it takes very aggressive policies

53:53.530 --> 54:00.290
to break those expectations and to get inflation up to the 2 percent target

54:00.290 --> 54:02.410
that the Bank of Japan has set.

54:02.410 --> 54:04.820
So that's why it's difficult, they've had to be very aggressive.

54:04.820 --> 54:08.520
That aggressiveness in the early stages of this process,

54:08.520 --> 54:13.950
where investors are still learning about the BOJ's reaction function,

54:13.950 --> 54:16.800
it's not all that surprising that there's volatility.

54:16.800 --> 54:21.080
Also, the JGB markets are less liquid than, say,

54:21.080 --> 54:23.060
the Treasury market, for example.

54:23.060 --> 54:26.100
So I-you know, I think that's something they need to pay close attention to,

54:26.100 --> 54:31.900
but, on the whole, I think that it is important for Japan to attack deflation.

54:31.900 --> 54:36.270
And I also agree with the "three arrows,"

54:36.270 --> 54:40.420
the idea that besides breaking deflation, it's important to address fiscal

54:40.420 --> 54:42.360
and structural issues as well.

54:42.360 --> 54:45.120
So, I'm supportive of my colleague, Mr. Kuroda,

54:45.120 --> 54:48.260
and I'm supportive of what Japan is doing,

54:48.260 --> 54:52.350
even though it does have some effects on our economy as well.

54:52.350 --> 54:58.570
The-there are a lot of reasons why emerging markets

54:58.570 --> 55:02.620
and other countries experience capital inflows and volatility.

55:02.620 --> 55:05.340
Some of them have to do with changes in growth expectations.

55:05.340 --> 55:08.160
For example, we've seen a lot of changes in growth patterns

55:08.160 --> 55:10.110
in the emerging markets recently.

55:10.110 --> 55:12.740
Some of it has to do with risk-on, risk-off behavior,

55:12.740 --> 55:16.290
and some of it probably does have to do with monetary policy

55:16.290 --> 55:19.000
in advanced economies, which includes, of course, the United States.

55:19.000 --> 55:23.900
We do take it-pay attention to that.

55:23.900 --> 55:28.020
I frequently meet with colleagues from emerging markets at the G 20,

55:28.020 --> 55:30.580
for example, and we discuss these issues.

55:30.580 --> 55:35.590
I think the right way to think about it is that,

55:35.590 --> 55:42.000
as the G-7 and the G-20 both have noted, that what U.S. monetary policy,

55:42.000 --> 55:47.080
like that of Japan, is trying to do is trying to help this economy grow.

55:47.080 --> 55:51.830
And a global recovery, a global-strong global growth depends very much

55:51.830 --> 55:55.130
on the U.S. growing at a reasonable rate.

55:55.130 --> 56:00.540
And so, while there is some effect, I think the net effect,

56:00.540 --> 56:03.710
including a stronger U.S. economy, is, on the whole, positive,

56:03.710 --> 56:07.600
and I think most of my colleagues in emerging markets recognize that.

56:07.600 --> 56:12.070
That being said, anything we can do through communication or other means to try

56:12.070 --> 56:22.590
to minimize any overflow effects or side effects, we will certainly do.

56:22.590 --> 56:24.980
MARK HAMRICK.

56:26.450 --> 56:29.910
Hello Mr. Chairman, Mark Hamrick with Bankrate.com.

56:29.910 --> 56:33.020
You know, you've talked about being in uncharted territory with policy,

56:33.020 --> 56:36.980
and we can remember that these news conferences just began about two years ago

56:36.980 --> 56:40.880
as well, and so there's that mix of communication and policy.

56:40.880 --> 56:44.760
Insofar as the FOMC decided not to include the information

56:44.760 --> 56:49.870
about adjusting the asset purchases today, how do you walk that fine line?

56:49.870 --> 56:53.590
And why did the FOMC decide essentially to leave it to you to describe that,

56:53.590 --> 56:56.050
as opposed to putting it down in its own words?

56:56.050 --> 56:56.300
Thank you.

56:56.300 --> 56:57.020
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

56:57.020 --> 57:03.820
Well, again, we don't think of this as a change in policy.

57:03.820 --> 57:08.130
What I was deputized to do, if you will,

57:08.130 --> 57:13.400
was to try to make somewhat clearer the implications of our existing policy

57:13.400 --> 57:20.570
and to try to explain better how the policy would evolve

57:20.570 --> 57:24.620
in various economic scenarios, and that's a little bit difficult to put into,

57:24.620 --> 57:27.810
you know, a very terse FOMC statement.

57:27.810 --> 57:31.860
Now that being said, going forward, I think that, you know,

57:31.860 --> 57:36.240
some of these elements, to the extent that we can make them useful,

57:36.240 --> 57:38.180
will begin to appear in the FOMC statement.

57:38.180 --> 57:39.620
It's entirely possible.

57:39.620 --> 57:44.040
But it seemed like the right tactic in this case

57:44.040 --> 57:51.460
to explain these fairly subtle contingencies in a context

57:51.460 --> 57:53.220
where I could answer questions

57:53.220 --> 57:58.330
and respond to any misunderstandings that might occur.

57:58.330 --> 57:59.380
Thank you.

